# Tutorial 18 — Plugins and Pikupiku
# Reference: docs/plugins/image-effects.md
# Read scripts/tutorial/README.md for the complete course and linked references.
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_18
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}Pikupiku{/b} is an optional native Java plugin port of the line wiggler. This tutorial keeps my original drawing and the title still.
Lavender: The plugin prepares a few drawings once, then holds each frame. You can try it in the editor's preview window when it suits your own project.
@label lesson_menu
> Compare presets in the editor -> compare
> Use the editor tool -> studio
> How plugins extend JVN -> authoring
> Checkpoint -> checkpoint
> Finish and return to the hub -> return_hub
@label compare
Lavender: In the Pikupiku tool, compare Subtle with Strong. Look at displacement, line width, and pressure; use Off to compare the original image.
narrator: Optional script example: [set character.lavender.imageEffect pikupiku.lineboil]
narrator: Optional parameter: [set character.lavender.imageEffect.strength 0.6]
Lavender: These are ordinary saved variables. To remove an effect, set its ID to {b}none{/b}. The example is printed here without applying it to the tutorial.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label studio
Lavender: Open {b}Tools > Plugins > Pikupiku Line Wiggler{/b}. Start with Lavender, or open your own PNG.
Lavender: Try the Lavender, Subtle, Strong, Graphite, Omori, and Off presets. Then tune displacement, timing, ink, pressure, seed, width, grain, smoothing, color, paper, and hatching.
Lavender: Copy VNS settings for your character. Export PNG drawings when you need baked frames; the export includes timing and the script settings, and keeps transparency.
Lavender: Exercise: make two visibly different presets, copy them into this file, and let a story choice select between them.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label authoring
Lavender: A plugin JAR declares its entrypoint, API range, dependencies, and capabilities in {b}jvn-plugin.json{/b}. Project plugins live in {b}plugins/{/b}; user plugins live in the JVN user data folder.
Lavender: API 1.2 adds {b}image.effect{/b}: typed pixels, validated parameters, presets, and prepared animation frames. The editor provides a native preview service so the plugin stays independent of JavaFX.
Lavender: Other families are script commands, editor tools, asset importers, runtime listeners, and animation easings. Registration handles can be closed individually; stopping a plugin removes its contributions.
Lavender: Capability checks organize access. Plugins are still executable Java with your process permissions: install code you trust. Reload uses a new host; save work before changing plugin JARs.
Lavender: Exercise: inspect {b}modules/plugin-pikupiku{/b} in the engine source, then implement an image effect with one parameter and a qualified ID such as mygame.outline.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label checkpoint
Lavender: Where should an expensive pixel transform run?
> Once on a worker, with prepared frames reused -> correct
> On every render pass -> retry
@label retry
Lavender: That can stall dialogue and input. Prepare bounded frames in the background, then reuse them.
[jump checkpoint]
@label correct
Lavender: Exactly. The host validates settings, limits cached frames, and falls back to the original image if an effect fails.
[jump return_hub]

@label return_hub
[set character.lavender.imageEffect.strength default]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect.fps default]
[if !tutorial_done_18]
[flag tutorial_done_18]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_4]
